                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>The occasional bout of heartburn (or Gastroesophageal Reflux)</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No, antacids are contraindicated when the condition of reflux becomes chronic (or longstanding) for reasons we will discuss soon.&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "tip": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GORD or GERD)</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No, another medication that we will discuss, the proton pump inhibitors will be the medication of choice. As we will soon see, the antacids result in a REBOUND hyperacidity and that will mean that they should not be used in people with peptic ulcers.</div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Peptic Ulcer Disease</div>\n"
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
